Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to design a secure, scalable IPC-backed ML pipeline with strict isolation and power-aware execution.

Key Concepts

- Android isolatedProcess and service lifecycle
- AIDL IPC contracts and security
- Memory budgeting and Doze-friendly execution
- On-device model loading from signed remote packages
- Side-channel resistance and data leakage risks

Code Example

```kotlin
// Pseudo-bounded service
class InferenceBinder : IInferenceAidl.Stub() {
  override fun runInference(input: FloatArray): FloatArray {
    // validate input, route to isolated model
    return model.infer(input)
  }
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you enforce a per-request memory budget at runtime?
- How do you detect and prevent timing side channels in inference results?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-process plug-in architectures, security boundaries, and robust IPC in Android.

## Key Concepts

- android:isolatedProcess, AIDL IPC, plugin sandboxing
- dynamic module loading, plugin signing, host verification
- data modeling for metadata and capabilities, lifecycle commands
- test strategy: IPC contracts, rollback, performance, security fuzzing

## Code Example

```java
// AIDL sketch (IPluginAidl.aidl)
interface IPluginAidl {
  void attach(IHostHost host);
  void detach();
  void render(FrameBuffer fb);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle plugin versioning and backward compatibility across host upgrades?
- What metrics and tooling would you use to monitor plugin health in production?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes hardening of dynamic extension systems: process isolation, signed module trust, and robust IPC with stable lifecycles.

## Key Concepts
- Android isolated processes and AIDL IPC
- Signed plugin discovery via PackageManager
- Per-extension sandboxed storage and crash containment
- Versioning, capability negotiation, and recovery

## Code Example
```aidl
// IExtensionAidl.aidl
interface IExtension {
  void initialize(in String configJson);
  java.util.List<String> getCapabilities();
  void reportEvent(in String eventJson);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle plugin upgrades and rollback without host downtime?
- How would you sandbox and revoke a misbehaving extension at runtime?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes understanding of Android process isolation and IPC, combined with dynamic config and per-user overrides. It also tests data modeling for flags and robust test planning.

## Key Concepts
- Android sandboxing with android:process and android:isolatedProcess
- AIDL IPC surface and interface stability
- Encrypted Room storage with Keystore-backed keys
- Per-user/session overrides and delta/config rollback

